{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take every change to agreed requirement text, including a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement, through the same review and agreement before committing it.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to put every change to agreed requirement text through the same review and agreement before committing it. The text names two cases: a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]` checks the bundled skill text.\n- `assert_phrases` requires the sentence \"Every change to agreed text, including a rewording for precision or removing a requirement, goes through steps 3 to 5 before it is committed\". It runs on the collapsed `bundled_skill` text, so line wrapping does not matter. The sentence names both cases the requirement lists, a rewording and a removal. It also fixes the timing as before the commit. `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` (Workflow section) contains exactly this sentence.\n- `step_of(\"habeas-requirements\", \"Sort the new and changed requirements into groups\")` must equal 3. This ties \"steps 3 to 5\" to the review sequence actually starting at step 3. If the steps were renumbered, the test would fail. A separate test pins the order draft, review, gate, commit and implement.\n- The checks that step 1 scopes the process to observable behavior and that the description triggers \"before changing code, tests, or requirement text\" support the direction to apply the process to changes. They are context for the obligation. The requirement does not need them.\n\n**Gaps.** The skill text is the only thing the requirement governs, and the assertions pin the directive in that text. I found no plausible violation that these assertions would accept. For example, dropping the removal case, dropping the rewording case, dropping the \"before it is committed\" timing, or renumbering the review step would each fail the test. The other assertions in the test do not matter for this requirement. I found no marking gap, because the test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject.",
  "references": "9854ef5544b06bd0e771922109f2946991072b56097f87b408baa38f84a68c16",
